      },
      "message": "The mutation arms bound their waits in DUT cycles, not host time, because both harnesses already do\n\nThe arms landed with a host deadline around each mutant run, which was\nbelt-and-braces for a hang that neither harness can actually produce. Both\nbound their own waits in DUT CYCLES: the gasket harness does it so that a\nwedged gasket FAILS the suite instead of hanging it - the very property\nthe first gasket mutant exercises - and tsngen puts an explicit tick\nceiling on every wait it performs.\n\nSo the deadline answered a question the DUT already answers, and answered\nit in a machine-dependent way: the same mutant could be a catch on one\nhost and a TIMEOUT on a slower one. A mutation arm whose verdict depends\non how fast the machine is cannot be evidence about the assertions.\n\nThe runs are now plain subprocess calls, the TIMEOUT branch and the\nprocess-group kill machinery are gone with the deadline that needed them,\nand the reason is recorded where the verdict is read. Both arms still\npass unchanged: the gasket arm at 5/5 and tsngen at 4/4, every mutant\ncaught and both positive controls passing.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01PeGPJEKDwL5BrX2E5PUTJT",
